# AbstractMachine (AM)

A minimal, modular, machine-independent hardware abstraction layer, originally from
[NJU-ProjectN/abstract-machine](https://github.com/NJU-ProjectN/abstract-machine).
Bug reports/suggestions for the original project go to its maintainers, Yanyan Jiang
(jyy@nju.edu.cn) and Zihao Yu (yuzihao@ict.ac.cn) — this is an unmodified copy, tracked
here purely as part of the [ysyx](../) course scaffold.

## What it provides

AM gives programs a single, consistent API regardless of what they're actually running
on — the same source code can target a real x86/RISC-V machine, an instruction-set
simulator (NEMU), or eventually a student's own processor (NPC), without changes.

It's split into five conceptual layers:

| Layer | Provides |
|---|---|
| **TRM** (Turing Machine) | Physical memory + direct instruction execution — the bare minimum to run *any* program |
| **IOE** (I/O Extension) | Basic input/output devices: timer, keyboard, display (GPU framebuffer), audio |
| **CTE** (Context Extension) | Interrupts, exceptions, processor context switching |
| **VME** (Virtual Memory Extension) | Virtual memory and memory protection |
| **MPE** (Multiprocessing Extension) | Multi-core/multiprocessing support |

## Key directories

```
abstract-machine/
├── am/include/          Public API headers (am.h, amdev.h, and per-arch headers)
├── am/src/<arch>/<platform>/   Per-target implementations (native, riscv/npc, riscv/nemu, ...)
├── klib/                 A minimal standard-library replacement (printf, malloc, string
│                          functions, ...) — deliberately scaled down so the same code
│                          can run even on bare-metal targets with no OS underneath
└── scripts/               Makefile fragments selecting ISA/platform per target
```

## How I've used this so far

- `ARCH=native` — compiling AM programs to run directly on my own Linux machine
  (fastest to iterate on; used for the screensaver and GUI-enabled `minirvEMU` in
  [am-kernels](https://github.com/graff1452/am-kernels))
- `scripts/minirv-npc.mk` and `am/src/riscv/npc/` already exist in this framework,
  meaning AM has built-in support for eventually targeting my own RTL processor
  (`npc/`, once it's further along) — no extra AM-side work needed to reach that point
